-- title:
--   Integrality at height-one primes with no pole centred at (a,a^q)
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ with decidable equality, a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red}\colon A\to k$, an element $a\in k$, and a finite extension $K/\mathbb Q$ inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}$. Let $C$ denote `jIntegralClosure (1 * q) A K`, the subring of Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ consisting of the elements of `fieldOver (1 * q) K` — the subfield generated by the constant series from $K$ together with the two series $j$ and $j_q$ — that are integral over `jRing A K`, and let $\mathfrak p\subset C$ be a prime ideal of height $1$. Assume: (h𝔭S) for every polynomial $s$ in two variables over $A\cap K$ whose reduction, evaluated at $(a,a^q)$ via $\mathrm{red}$, is nonzero, the series $s(j,j_q)$ does not lie in $\mathfrak p$ whenever it lies in $C$. Let $g$ lie in `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)`, the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the full modular function field of level $q$, with (hg) $g\in$ `fieldOver (1 * q) K`, and suppose (h₁) $g$ and (h₂) its image under the Fricke involution `frickeInvolutionBar (1 * q)` both lie in the localisation `CharPReduction.modularLocalized (1 * q) A.toSubring red` of the modular ring at the kernel of $\mathrm{red}$. Assume finally (hpole) that for every place $W$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\operatorname{ord}_W g<0$ it is not the case that both there is $x\in A$ with $\mathrm{red}\,x=a$ and $\operatorname{ord}_W(j-x)>0$, and there is $y\in A$ with $\mathrm{red}\,y=a^q$ and $\operatorname{ord}_W(j_q-y)>0$. Then there exist $r,s\in C$ with $s\notin\mathfrak p$ and $g\cdot s=r$.
--
--   This states that a modular function of level $q$ which is integral at the two boundary components (itself and its Fricke transform lying in the localised modular ring) and has no pole at a place centred at the point $(a,a^q)$ of the special fibre belongs to the localisation of the normalised node ring at every height-one prime through that point. It is the form in which the classification of such height-one primes is used, and feeds the integrality statements [`ModularCurve.isIntegral_modularLocalizedAtPoint_coeffSubring_of_forall_pole_not_centred`](thm.html#ModularCurve.isIntegral_modularLocalizedAtPoint_coeffSubring_of_forall_pole_not_centred) and [`ModularCurve.isIntegral_modularLocalizedAtPoint_of_forall_ord_nonneg`](thm.html#ModularCurve.isIntegral_modularLocalizedAtPoint_of_forall_ord_nonneg).
